######
n a traditional RC-triggered ESD clamp, the waveforms at each node represent the circuit's ability to distinguish between a fast ESD event and a slow normal power-up
. The typical behavior at key nodes during an ESD event (e.g., a 10 ns rise time pulse) is as follows: 

    VDD (Power Rail): Under an ESD event, the rail voltage rapidly spikes (e.g., to ~5V–6V for an HBM event). The clamp's goal is to keep this voltage below the gate-oxide breakdown limit of internal core transistors.
    Detector Node (between R and C): Because the ESD transient is much faster than the RC time constant (typically designed for ~100ns–1µs), the capacitor acts as a short circuit initially, causing this node to remain near ground (VSS).
    Inverter Output / Gate Node: The low voltage at the detector node is inverted by the buffer stage, driving the gate of the large Mclamp (BigFET) high. This turns the transistor ON to shunt the ESD current from VDD to VSS.
    Clamp Transistor (Mclamp): The current through the clamp rises instantly to several Amperes (e.g., ~1.33A for a 2kV HBM) and then decays as the ESD energy is dissipated. 

Node Waveform Comparison
Node      Normal      Power-Up (Slow)	    ESD Event (Fast)
VDD Rail	Gradual rise to operating voltage.	Rapid transient spike.
RC Node	  Follows VDD; capacitor has time to charge.	Remains low; capacitor cannot charge instantly.
Gate Node	Remains low; Mclamp stays OFF.	Driven high; Mclamp turns ON.
Current (I)	Minimal leakage (nA range).	High discharge current (Amperes).
Traditional designs may suffer from oscillation if the VDD rail drops below the inverter trip point before the ESD energy is fully depleted, causing the Mclamp to prematurely turn off and then back on. Modern 2026 designs often replace the simple RC with feedback-enhanced or thyristor-delay elements to ensure the clamp stays on for the full duration (~1µs) of an HBM event regardless of rail fluctuations. 
